        "type": "tutorial",
        "title": "Summation Notation and Basic Rules",
        "slug": "summation-notation-and-basic-rules",
        "welcome": "Adding up the terms of a sequence is so common that mathematicians invented a compact notation: the BIG SIGMA ∑.",
        "objectives": [
          "Read and write a summation in ∑ notation, identifying index, bounds, and summand.",
          "Apply the constant-factor rule (pull constants out).",
          "Apply the sum-of-a-constant rule: ∑ k = k · (count of terms).",
          "Apply linearity to split sums.",
          "Apply the splitting rule to change bounds."
        ],
        "storytelling": [
          {
            "speaker": "Student",
            "text": "What does ∑_{i=1}^{5} i² mean exactly?"
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Professor",
            "text": "It is shorthand for 1² + 2² + 3² + 4² + 5². The `i` is the INDEX, 1 is the LOWER bound, 5 is the UPPER bound, and i² is the SUMMAND. Read it as a for-loop."
          }
        ],
        "flow": [
          {
            "type": "definition",
            "title": "Summation",
            "content": "**∑_{i=m}^{n} f(i)** = f(m) + f(m+1) + … + f(n). Four ingredients: index `i`, lower bound `m`, upper bound `n`, summand `f(i)`. If n < m, the sum is empty (= 0)."
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "title": "Quick examples",
            "headers": [
              "Expression",
              "Expansion",
              "Value"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "∑_{i=1}^{4} i",
                "1 + 2 + 3 + 4",
                "10"
              ],
              [
                "∑_{k=0}^{3} 2ᵏ",
                "1 + 2 + 4 + 8",
                "15"
              ],
              [
                "∑_{j=1}^{n} 1",
                "1 + 1 + … + 1 (n terms)",
                "n"
              ],
              [
                "∑_{i=5}^{4} f(i)",
                "(empty)",
                "0"
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "definition",
            "title": "Constant-factor rule",
            "content": "If `c` does NOT depend on the index, pull it out: `∑_{i=m}^{n} c · f(i) = c · ∑_{i=m}^{n} f(i)`. Only constants can leave the ∑."
          },
          {
            "type": "warning",
            "title": "Don't pull the index out",
            "content": "You can pull `5` out of `∑ 5·i`, but you CANNOT pull `i` out of `∑ i · f(i)` because `i` changes each step."
          },
          {
            "type": "definition",
            "title": "Sum of a constant",
            "content": "**∑_{i=m}^{n} k = k · (n - m + 1)**. Special case: ∑_{i=1}^{n} 1 = n; ∑_{i=1}^{n} k = kn."
          },
          {
            "type": "definition",
            "title": "Linearity (sum of a sum)",
            "content": "**∑ (f(i) + g(i)) = ∑ f(i) + ∑ g(i)**, and similarly for subtraction. Combined with the constant-factor rule, this gives full linearity: `∑ (a · f(i) + b · g(i)) = a · ∑ f(i) + b · ∑ g(i)`."
          },
          {
            "type": "definition",
            "title": "Splitting rule (changing the lower bound)",
            "content": "**∑_{i=m}^{n} f(i) = ∑_{i=1}^{n} f(i) - ∑_{i=1}^{m-1} f(i)**. Useful when standard formulas (Lesson 2) require lower bound 1."
          },
          {
            "type": "real_life_example",
            "title": "Sigma in real life",
            "items": [
              "**Total CPCS 222 attendance** = ∑_{i=1}^{n} aᵢ where aᵢ = 1 if present on day i.",
              "**Total monthly STC bill** for a year = ∑_{i=1}^{12} bᵢ.",
              "**Total study hours** in a semester = ∑_{i=1}^{14} hᵢ.",
              "**Total parking-fee installments**: 12 monthly payments at SAR 200 each = ∑_{i=1}^{12} 200 = 2400."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "worked_example",
            "title": "Use the rules without closed forms",
            "source_pattern": "Inspired by Lab 08.",
            "problem": "Use linearity and the constant rule to simplify ∑_{i=1}^{n} (3 + 2i) without yet using closed forms.",
            "thinking_process": "Linearity, then sum-of-constant, then constant-factor.",
            "step_by_step_solution": [
              "Linearity: ∑(3 + 2i) = ∑3 + ∑2i.",
              "Sum of constant: ∑3 = 3n.",
              "Constant factor: ∑2i = 2 · ∑i."
            ],
            "final_answer": "3n + 2 · ∑_{i=1}^{n} i.",
            "common_mistake": "Forgetting that ∑_{i=1}^{n} 3 = 3n (not just 3)."
          },
          {
            "type": "worked_example",
            "title": "Splitting the bound",
            "source_pattern": "Sec 2.4 splitting.",
            "problem": "Rewrite ∑_{i=4}^{12} f(i) using bounds starting at 1.",
            "thinking_process": "Apply splitting rule.",
            "step_by_step_solution": [
              "∑_{i=4}^{12} f(i) = ∑_{i=1}^{12} f(i) - ∑_{i=1}^{3} f(i)."
            ],
            "final_answer": "∑_{i=1}^{12} f(i) - ∑_{i=1}^{3} f(i).",
            "common_mistake": "Subtracting ∑_{i=1}^{4} instead of ∑_{i=1}^{3}."
          },
          {
            "type": "misconception_box",
            "title": "Common student mistakes",
            "items": [
              "Pulling out the index variable. Only constants leave ∑.",
              "∑ k = k (one occurrence) instead of k · (count).",
              "Off-by-one in (n - m + 1) count.",
              "Trying to use linearity over multiplication. ∑(f · g) ≠ ∑f · ∑g."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "memory_hook",
            "title": "Three quick rules",
            "items": [
              "**Pull constants out** (no index dependence).",
              "**Sum of a constant** = constant × count.",
              "**Linearity** splits ∑(f + g) into ∑f + ∑g."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "simulator_sigma_expand",
            "title": "Try It — Summation Expander"
          }
        ],
        "sections": [
