            "speaker": "Student",
            "text": "Is there a faster way than computing each term in ∑_{i=1}^{100} i?"
          },
          {
            "speaker": "Professor",
            "text": "Yes — Gauss's formula: n(n+1)/2. With n = 100 that gives 5050. One line. Memorize four formulas of this kind and you can collapse any standard sum into a single algebraic expression."
          }
        ],
        "flow": [
          {
            "type": "table",
            "title": "Four closed-form formulas to memorize",
            "headers": [
              "Formula",
              "Closed form",
              "Mnemonic"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "∑_{i=1}^{n} 1",
                "n",
                "Just n copies"
              ],
              [
                "∑_{i=1}^{n} i",
                "n(n+1)/2",
                "Gauss's pair-up trick"
              ],
              [
                "∑_{i=1}^{n} i²",
                "n(n+1)(2n+1)/6",
                "Sum of squares"
              ],
              [
                "∑_{i=1}^{n} i³",
                "(n(n+1)/2)² = (∑i)²",
                "Cubes = square of sum"
              ]
            ]
          },
